                "text": "**Analyzing the Transformation**\n\nI'm currently dissecting a data transformation. Initial input data has 16 rows and 8 columns. Column 0 is uniformly zero. Columns 1-7 mostly feature the value seven with some eights interspersed. For instance, Row 3 has 2 eights, while Row 5 has 1 eight. I am continuing to break this down to understand the patterns involved.\n\n\n**Observing Output Shifts**\n\nI've been looking closely at the data transformations, focusing now on how the \"8\" values are handled. In output 1, the number of 8s is preserved, but moved to the end, becoming 0. In output 2, it seems if the original column contains 8s, in the output, this becomes 2 rows that have 8! I need to determine the rule for preserving the number of 8s.\n\n\n**Defining the Parameters**\n\nI've made a breakthrough. I've realized that 0 acts as a \"border\" or \"wall\", with 7 as the background. The 8s \"shoot\" out from the border, and their length is key to how they're transformed. In train 1, 8s' growth and \"shooting\" across is tied to the width. In train 2, the same logic holds, but vertically. Now I'm checking with test input 1, which has size 8x16.\n\n\n**Interpreting the Growth**\n\nI've been analyzing the test input columns, and have now determined that the base, the value 0, is located in row 7, which acts as the bottom. I noticed that 8s grow upwards, starting from row 6, then continuing to row 5, and so on. Now I am using the concept of length, based on the number of 8s, to predict what happens to each column. I've separated them into two categories: length 1 columns and length 2 columns.\n\n\n**Predicting Column Changes**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on predicting column transformations. Length-2 columns in test input 1 have confirmed a pattern: the \"opposite end\" rows 0 and 1 become zeros, while rows 5 and 6 become sevens. I've validated this logic across train and test sets, noting the impact of base location, length, and \"opposite end\" definitions to formulate a matrix output. My next step is filling out the output matrix.\n\n\n",
